  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Radius of the Bohr&#39;s orbit is calculated by the following formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.529</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8491;</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace></math></p><p>Radius is directly proportional to&nbsp;square of&nbsp;n<sup>th</sup> orbit and inversely proportional to atomic number. This formula works for single electron species</p><p>here&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></math>radius of the nth orbit</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo></math>orbit or shell number<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.529</mo><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">2</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;&#8491;</mo></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.12&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#8491;</mi></math></p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
